## Finishing Halcyon

Since the sprite experiments, Halcyon has become a complete scheme of 329 resources. Beyond frames, buttons and checkboxes it themes scroll bars, sliders, tabs, bevel and popup buttons, progress bars (a dithered barber pole), list headers, disclosure triangles, menus, a desktop pattern and its own icon in the scheme picker. Real Kaleidoscope draws the menus, desktop pattern, picker icon, list view, scroll bars, tabs and popup buttons from it. Finder icons, cursors, sounds and accent colours are still the defaults.

The control families were built by four AI agents working in parallel, one per family, each writing its own module against the specs Scriptoscope had already decoded. A fifth agent ran the emulator.

### Anti-aliasing with a 1-bit mask

I asked for better anti-aliasing and dithering, and that changed the toolkit more than I expected. A 1-bit mask cannot be half transparent, so the smooth shapes blend each edge pixel by how much of it the shape covers: either with the pixel underneath, or with a "matte" colour the art normally sits on, which for Halcyon is the white window body. Ordered (Bayer) dithering gives smooth gradients in very few colours.

<figure class="kh-figure">
  <img class="kh-figure__image" src="/images/blog/halcyon-scroll-arrow-anti-aliased.png" width="1400" eleventy:formats="png" alt="A five-times zoom of Halcyon's up scroll arrow in four states, labelled normal, up, down and page. Each is a dark grey chevron on a pale grey-to-white gradient; the pressed state is a white chevron on a teal gradient. The chevron edges are made of intermediate grey or teal pixels that soften the diagonals.">
  <figcaption class="kh-figure__caption">A scroll arrow at 5×. The in-between pixels on the chevron are coverage blends, since the mask itself can only be on or off.</figcaption>
</figure>

The gotcha: never dither art the engine stretches or tiles along one axis. A dithered column repeated across a scroll track turns into stripes. Patterns that tile both ways, like the desktop, can be dithered safely.

### The real engine is the best checker

Real Kaleidoscope caught a bug our checks did not. Tab-pane labels were drawn white on white, because the text colour was read from the same pixel as the background. It is fixed, confirmed in a final boot, and there is now a check for it.

<figure class="kh-figure">
  <img class="kh-figure__image" src="/images/blog/halcyon-kaleidoscope-control-panel.png" width="1280" eleventy:formats="png" alt="The Kaleidoscope 2.3.1 control panel on System 7.5.5 with the Halcyon scheme selected. Two tabs, Scheme Settings and General Options, have dark, readable labels. Below are checkboxes for scheme options with Desktop Pattern checked in teal, a greyed Accent Color popup set to Lavender, and Register and Select Scheme buttons.">
  <figcaption class="kh-figure__caption">The Kaleidoscope control panel after the fix, with readable tab labels.</figcaption>
</figure>

It worked the other way too. A plausible checker rule, "scroll pieces must be opaque", fired on 12 of 18 original 1990s schemes. It is a note now, not an error: the originals define what is valid.

## Gotchas running agy headless

If you script `agy -p` (print mode): tools are auto-denied, though it still tried to run a shell command after I told it not to. Despite `--json-schema`, the JSON sometimes arrived twice or wrapped in a `` ```json `` fence, so parse defensively. Print mode exits before subagents finish unless told to wait, and `--mode auto` is unavailable on some accounts.

## What I did not try

These need keys or subscriptions, so for now this is reading, not testing:

- **Retro Diffusion** is the only image model I found that takes exact small sizes (12 to 512 pixels), with `remove_bg` and a palette lock. Pricing is roughly $0.03 per image for fast, $0.06 for plus and $0.18 for pro, and their pixel fixer is $0.002. See their [API examples](https://github.com/Retro-Diffusion/api-examples)
- **PixelLab** has a pixel-art model on subscription. Most of its tools start at 32 pixels, which is bigger than most Kaleidoscope controls, and I have not verified its prices
- **OpenRouter** has about 20 general image models, at roughly 1 to 13 cents an image according to [OpenRouter's own September 2026 comparison](https://openrouter.ai/blog/insights/image-generation-models-compared/). Outputs start at 1,024 pixels and I could not find transparency documented, so I would expect the same grid problem as route A
- **Hugging Face Inference Providers** pass provider costs through, and a PRO account includes $2 a month of credits ([pricing](https://huggingface.co/docs/inference-providers/pricing)). There are community pixel-art LoRAs, but I did not find a priced hosted endpoint for one

Other Agent Skills exist: a Retro Diffusion skill in [PlayableIntelligence/game-creator](https://github.com/PlayableIntelligence/game-creator), and several Nano Banana skills, which are general image generation with the same grid problem.

Things still on my list: trials of Retro Diffusion and PixelLab, feeding the checker's errors back to the model in a loop, generating a whole window frame rather than single controls, and running more than two models on route C.

## What I am taking from this

For exact-format pixel art, ask the model for code or text grids, not pictures. An image model aims for a picture of pixel art; text makes it decide each pixel, and what comes back stays editable. Image models are still useful for mood boards. If you must use one for the asset, prompt for a flat key-colour background, run proper-pixel-art, and expect to fix the size yourself. And boot the real thing: the original engine caught what my checks missed.

If you have tried Retro Diffusion or PixelLab on very small sprites, or you have a Kaleidoscope scheme you would like to see in a browser, I would like to hear about it. Halcyon and the authoring toolkit are in [PR #540](https://github.com/khawkins98/scriptoscope/pull/540).

